# Copy to `.bazelrc.user` to add settings that are yours rather than the
# project's. The file is git-ignored, and `.bazelrc` imports it last so
# anything here wins. Nothing in it is required: the project builds without it.

# How much of this machine a build may take. Bazel schedules on core count and
# assumes no memory budget, so on a box with more cores than memory-per-core a
# build can outgrow the machine.
# build --local_resources=cpu=HOST_CPUS*0.7
# build --local_resources=memory=HOST_RAM*0.5

# A cache shared across checkouts, so a second clone reuses the first's work.
# It grows to whatever bound it is given and needs one.
# build --disk_cache=~/.cache/bazel-disk

# A BuildBuddy key, which `--config=ci` and `--config=rbe` need and neither
# defines. Both configs work only for someone who has an account; the project
# never requires one.
# build --remote_header=x-buildbuddy-api-key=YOUR_KEY_HERE

# Applies a config to every build without naming it on the command line. Under
# `rbe` the compiler is the remote image's, not the one `.bazelrc` names.
# build --config=rbe
